{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 demands a distribution-shift generalization test as the mechanism to distinguish genuine structural alignment from incidental correlation. But for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, the single evaluation can identify a genuine reliability gain by comparing the panel's performance against the expert's oracle-graded outcomes on held-out cases at the decision boundary. A causal-mechanism stance must explain *why* the panel gets the right answer; SRC_001's proposed shift test only shows that the panel fails under distribution shift, which does not identify the mechanism producing reliability in-distribution. Without identifying the within-distribution causal mechanism (e.g., does the checklist track outcome-discriminative features used by the true generative process?), the shift test alone cannot distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance that happens to track the right answer within the evaluated distribution.","target":"27529970a74774c8d3eba40bd7a324f6425a9442dcc664579f0895b2627ac246"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's central criterion for distinguishing genuine reliability gain from mere compliance within a single evaluation relies on the variance of panel members' individual judgments: low variance allegedly indicates proxy-based compliance forcing agreement, while genuine preservation of the expert's decision structure produces variance. But this prediction lacks a causal mechanism: SRC_002 never explains *why* a checklist that preserves the expert's causal structure would yield higher inter-member variance. The disanalogy SRC_002 itself notes — that a shared checklist forces perfectly correlated errors if flawed — implies the checklist constrains all members identically, so variance reflects the protocol's interpretive ambiguity, not whether the checklist captures the generative mechanism. A checklist encoding the true causal features could still be applied deterministically, yielding zero variance; conversely, a flawed proxy checklist could produce high variance if items are ambiguous. SRC_002 thus cannot show that high variance is the signature of genuine structural preservation rather than of ambiguous proxy wording.","counterexample":["A checklist encoding the true causal features is written so unambiguously that every trained non-expert applies it identically, producing zero variance while achieving genuine reliability.","A checklist encoding only surface proxies is written ambiguously, causing panel members to interpret items differently and produce high variance while merely tracking the right answer incidentally."],"target":"e2e7d6b93ae72267573841699576338b786e6e42ab3bdd5746c3c499af9c2509"}]}